@module flutter-m08 | Networking
@summary Talk to REST APIs with http and Dio, parse JSON and handle failures.

@lesson flutter-m08-l01 | HTTP fundamentals | 8
## Learning objectives
- Understand requests, responses and status codes

## HTTP fundamentals
HTTP is request/response: a method (GET, POST...), a URL, headers and an optional body go out; a status code, headers and a body come back. 2xx means success, 4xx a client error (401 unauthorised, 404 not found), 5xx a server error. Android needs the INTERNET permission in the manifest.

```dart
import 'package:http/http.dart' as http;

Future<void> main() async {
  final res = await http.get(Uri.parse('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/todos/1'));
  print('${res.statusCode} ${res.body}');
}
```

## Summary
- Check status codes before using the body.

@lesson flutter-m08-l02 | REST APIs | 8
## Learning objectives
- Map resources to endpoints

## REST APIs
REST APIs expose resources at URLs: `GET /lessons` lists, `GET /lessons/42` fetches one, `POST /lessons` creates, `PUT/PATCH /lessons/42` updates and `DELETE /lessons/42` removes. Wrap them in a repository class so widgets never build URLs.

```dart
import 'package:http/http.dart' as http;

class LessonApi {
  LessonApi(this.client, {this.base = 'https://api.example.com'});
  final http.Client client;
  final String base;
  Future<http.Response> list() => client.get(Uri.parse('$base/lessons'));
}
```

## Summary
- Keep endpoints inside an API/repository class.

@lesson flutter-m08-l03 | GET and POST | 8
## Learning objectives
- Fetch and create data

## GET and POST
GET reads data; POST sends a JSON body to create something. Set `Content-Type: application/json` and encode the body with `jsonEncode`.

```dart
import 'dart:convert';
import 'package:http/http.dart' as http;

Future<int> createNote(String title) async {
  final res = await http.post(
    Uri.parse('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts'),
    headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/json'},
    body: jsonEncode({'title': title}),
  );
  return res.statusCode; // 201 Created
}
```

## Summary
- POST bodies are usually JSON-encoded maps.

@lesson flutter-m08-l04 | PUT, PATCH and DELETE | 8
## Learning objectives
- Update and remove resources

## PUT, PATCH and DELETE
PUT replaces a resource, PATCH updates selected fields and DELETE removes it. Treat 200/204 as success.

```dart
import 'package:http/http.dart' as http;

Future<bool> deleteNote(int id) async {
  final res = await http.delete(Uri.parse('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/$id'));
  return res.statusCode == 200 || res.statusCode == 204;
}
```

## Summary
- PATCH for partial updates, PUT for full replacement.

@lesson flutter-m08-l05 | JSON parsing | 8
## Learning objectives
- Decode JSON into Dart maps and lists

## JSON parsing
`jsonDecode` turns a JSON string into `Map<String, dynamic>`/`List<dynamic>`. Convert those into typed models immediately.

```dart
import 'dart:convert';

void main() {
  final map = jsonDecode('{"id": 1, "title": "Learn"}') as Map<String, dynamic>;
  print(map['title']);
}
```

## Summary
- Decode once, convert to typed models right away.

@lesson flutter-m08-l06 | Serialization | 8
## Learning objectives
- Write fromJson/toJson model classes

## Serialization
Model classes with `fromJson` and `toJson` keep JSON handling in one place. For many models, generate them with `json_serializable` or `freezed`.

```dart
class Todo {
  Todo({required this.id, required this.title, required this.done});
  factory Todo.fromJson(Map<String, dynamic> j) => Todo(id: j['id'] as int, title: j['title'] as String, done: j['completed'] as bool);
  final int id;
  final String title;
  final bool done;
  Map<String, dynamic> toJson() => {'id': id, 'title': title, 'completed': done};
}
```

## Summary
- Code generation removes JSON boilerplate.

@lesson flutter-m08-l07 | Error handling | 8
## Learning objectives
- Handle network and server failures

## Error handling
Network calls fail: no connection (`SocketException`), timeouts, 4xx/5xx responses and malformed JSON. Catch them in the repository and return a typed error the UI can show.

```dart
import 'dart:io';
import 'package:http/http.dart' as http;

Future<String> load() async {
  try {
    final res = await http.get(Uri.parse('https://example.com/data'));
    if (res.statusCode != 200) return 'Server error ${res.statusCode}';
    return res.body;
  } on SocketException {
    return 'You are offline';
  }
}
```

## Summary
- Translate failures into user-friendly messages.

@lesson flutter-m08-l08 | Timeouts | 8
## Learning objectives
- Avoid waiting forever

## Timeouts
Always set a timeout on requests so the UI can recover.

```dart
import 'dart:async';
import 'package:http/http.dart' as http;

Future<http.Response> fetch(Uri url) => http.get(url).timeout(const Duration(seconds: 10));
```

## Summary
- Use .timeout and catch TimeoutException.

@lesson flutter-m08-l09 | Dio | 8
## Learning objectives
- Use Dio for interceptors and advanced features

## Dio
`dio` adds interceptors, base options, cancellation, form data and progress callbacks, and decodes JSON automatically.

```dart
import 'package:dio/dio.dart';

final dio = Dio(BaseOptions(baseUrl: 'https://api.example.com', connectTimeout: const Duration(seconds: 10)));

Future<List<dynamic>> lessons() async => (await dio.get<List<dynamic>>('/lessons')).data ?? [];
```

## Summary
- Dio suits apps with many endpoints and cross-cutting logic.

@lesson flutter-m08-l10 | Authentication tokens | 8
## Learning objectives
- Send bearer tokens with requests

## Authentication tokens
APIs often require `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. Add it in one place (a Dio interceptor) and store tokens in secure storage.

```dart
import 'package:dio/dio.dart';

void addAuth(Dio dio, String Function() token) {
  dio.interceptors.add(InterceptorsWrapper(onRequest: (options, handler) {
    options.headers['Authorization'] = 'Bearer ${token()}';
    handler.next(options);
  }));
}
```

## Summary
- Attach tokens centrally; refresh them on 401.

@lesson flutter-m08-l11 | Pagination | 8
## Learning objectives
- Load long lists page by page

## Pagination
APIs return data in pages (`?page=2` or a cursor). Load the next page when the user scrolls near the end and stop when a page is empty.

```dart
class Pager<T> {
  Pager(this.fetch);
  final Future<List<T>> Function(int page) fetch;
  final items = <T>[];
  int _page = 1;
  bool hasMore = true;
  Future<void> next() async {
    if (!hasMore) return;
    final batch = await fetch(_page++);
    hasMore = batch.isNotEmpty;
    items.addAll(batch);
  }
}
```

## Summary
- Track page and hasMore; avoid duplicate loads.

@lesson flutter-m08-l12 | Retry strategies | 8
## Learning objectives
- Retry transient failures with backoff

## Retry strategies
Retry only idempotent requests and transient errors (timeouts, 5xx), with exponential backoff: wait 1 s, 2 s, 4 s...

```dart
Future<T> retry<T>(Future<T> Function() task, {int attempts = 3}) async {
  var delay = const Duration(seconds: 1);
  for (var i = 1; ; i++) {
    try {
      return await task();
    } catch (_) {
      if (i >= attempts) rethrow;
      await Future.delayed(delay);
      delay *= 2;
    }
  }
}
```

## Summary
- Back off exponentially and cap attempts.
